"More on discrete convexity - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X2600051X)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Volume 385, 31 May 2026, Pages 146-164# More on discrete convexity ... We study some discrete objects that share this property and provide several examples of convex families related to graphs and to two-person games in normal form.\n\n## Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 385, 31 May 2026, Pages 146-164\n\n# More on discrete convexity\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2026.01.028Get rights and content\n\nUnder a Creative Commons license\n\nOpen access\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn several recent papers some concepts of convex analysis were extended to discrete sets. The present paper is one more step in this direction. It is well known that a local minimum of a convex function is always its global minimum. We study some discrete objects that share this property and provide several examples of convex families related to graphs and to two-person games in normal form.\n\n\n## Keywords\n\nConvex\n\nConnected\n\nGraph\n\nPerfect graphs\n\nKernel\n\nGame\n\nTwo-person game in normal form\n\nSaddle point\n\nNash equilibrium\n\n## Data availability\n\nNo data was used for the research described in the article.\n\n## Cited by (0)\n\n© 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterising and Detecting Sponsored Inﬂuencer (https://servicearchitecture.wp.imtbs-tsp.eu/files/2020/12/2011.05757.pdf)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; The concept of social inﬂuence is a well studied topic. ... graph properties of these users, to ﬁnd that inﬂuencers have ... that these targeted studies likely only capture a small slice\nThere have also been a small set of studies that rely on\ncomputational datasets. Lahuerta et al. [13] studied the role\nof marketing inﬂuencers in the Japanese automotive sector.\nThey collected 30K tweets based on relevant keywords. They\nfound, amongst other things, that the sentiment and length\nof inﬂuencer tweets has a positive effect on popularity. Al-\nthough highly relevant to our work, it is unclear how these\ntrends generalise across other brands and large-scale inﬂuencer\nnetworks. Closest to our work is [14], which explored the\nfollower graphs of inﬂuencers on Instagram. They targeted 218\nspeciﬁc social inﬂuencers (with 8.9M followers) as registered\nvia Popular Pays, a platform used to connecting brands to\ninﬂuencers. These were then compared against 948 Instagram\nusers with fewer than 10K followers. They studied the social\ngraph properties of these users, to ﬁnd that inﬂuencers have\nlarge numbers of followers, and even tend to interconnect\namongst themselves. They also found that users often follow\nmultiple inﬂuencers. Although highly applicable, we argue\nthat these targeted studies likely only capture a small slice--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOne-Cut Risk Profiles under Quadratic Loss: Discrete Convexity, Continuous Limits, and Higher Dimensions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05357)\nciteturn3academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: One-Cut Risk Profiles under Quadratic Loss: Discrete Convexity, Continuous Limits, and Higher Dimensions ... Date: Fri Sep  4 17:02:42 2026 ... On an equally spaced support, log-concavity gives this convexity, while weak symmetry locates the optimal cut, with an additional correction when the mean lies between two atoms.\nTitle: One-Cut Risk Profiles under Quadratic Loss: Discrete Convexity, Continuous Limits, and Higher Dimensions\nAuthors: Mihaela-Adriana Nistor, Ionel Popescu\nDate: Fri Sep  4 17:02:42 2026\n\nIn this note we study a two-regime representation of a loss random variable under quadratic error. For a finite law we compute exactly the change of the optimal risk when one atom crosses the cut. This turns the problem into a convexity question in cumulative-mass coordinates. On an equally spaced support, log-concavity gives this convexity, while weak symmetry locates the optimal cut, with an additional correction when the mean lies between two atoms. We also discuss the continuous analogue and extend the main identities to finitely supported random vectors, where a global optimal partition may be chosen as a halfspace.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTandem concavity with application to matching problems - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167637725001415)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Date: January 2026 ... Every function expressed as a lexicographic composition of two ordinally concave functions satisfies tandem concavity. ... Discrete convexity\n\nDate: January 2026\n\nArticle: 107380\n\nVolume: Volume 64\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nShort communication\n\nOpen access\n\nUnder a Creative Commons license\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# Tandem concavity with application to matching problems\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Satoru Fujishige ^{a}, Koji Yokote ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\n10.1016/j.orl.2025.107380\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nAs a generalization of ordinal concavity we introduce a new notion of discrete concavity called tandem concavity defined for a function over the subsets of a finite set E endowed with an ordered partition . Every function expressed as a lexicographic composition of two ordinally concave functions satisfies tandem concavity. We apply tandem concavity to the rationalization of choice rules in stable matching problems. We show that tandem concavity rationalizes a wider class of choice rules than ordinal concavity.\n\n## MSC\n\n90C27\n\n;\n\n06A07\n\n;\n\n91B14\n\n;\n\n91B68\n\n## Keywords\n\nDiscrete convexity\n\n;\n\nOrdinal concavity\n\n;\n\nTandem concavity\n\n;\n\nStable matchings\n\n## Data availability--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUTVPI-representable integer point sets: discrete convexity, polymorphisms, and pairwise closure (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24078)\nciteturn3academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Tue Aug 25 05:19:19 2026 ... We relate five viewpoints: inequality representation, discrete convexity, polymorphisms, reconstruction from two-coordinate projections, and fixed points of closure operators. ... For every set $S\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^n$ with $n>1$, \\[ \\begin{aligned} &S\\text{ is UTVPI-representable}\\\\ &\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; S\\text{ is closed under the directed midpoint and median operations}\\\\ &\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; S\\text{ is integrally convex and $2$-decomposable}. \\end{aligned} \\] The median condition may instead be replaced by closedness under some majority operation, and the same class is the fixed-point class of a pairwise directed-midpoint closure operator.\nTitle: UTVPI-representable integer point sets: discrete convexity, polymorphisms, and pairwise closure\nAuthors: Kei Kimura, Kazuhisa Makino, Shota Yamada, Ryo Yoshizumi\nDate: Tue Aug 25 05:19:19 2026\n\nWe study subsets of the integer lattice represented by single-variable-per-inequality (SVPI), difference-constraint (DC), unit two-variable-per-inequality (UTVPI), and two-variable-per-inequality (TVPI) systems. We relate five viewpoints: inequality representation, discrete convexity, polymorphisms, reconstruction from two-coordinate projections, and fixed points of closure operators. Our central result completely characterizes UTVPI-representability. For every set $S\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^n$ with $n>1$, \\[ \\begin{aligned} &S\\text{ is UTVPI-representable}\\\\ &\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; S\\text{ is closed under the directed midpoint and median operations}\\\\ &\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; S\\text{ is integrally convex and $2$-decomposable}. \\end{aligned} \\] The median condition may instead be replaced by closedness under some majority operation, and the same class is the fixed-point class of a pairwise directed-midpoint closure operator. Thus, all five viewpoints yield equivalent characterizations of UTVPI-representability. In particular, $2$-decomposability is exactly the global condition needed to lift the known two-dimensional equivalence between integral convexity and UTVPI-representability to arbitrary dimension. This theorem is embedded in a broader pairwise-closure theory. For a family $F$ of operations, we define a closure operator by closing every two-coordinate projection under $F$ and joining the resulting sets. Its fixed points are precisely the sets that are both $2$-decomposable and $F$-closed, and we establish a local-to-global criterion for such characterizations. A closed-convex-hull analogue characterizes TVPI-representability. We also characterize SVPI-representability by natural multioperations, prove limitations of operation-based characterizations for several related classes, and determine the complete inclusion hierarchies in the general, Boolean, and two-dimensional settings.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExact generalized Turán number of vertex-disjoint paths of length two (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.18122)\nciteturn3academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Title: Exact generalized Turán number of vertex-disjoint paths of length two ... Date: Mon Jul 20 16:16:56 2026 ... Our proof combines the Lovász form of the Kruskal--Katona theorem with a discrete convexity argument.\nTitle: Exact generalized Turán number of vertex-disjoint paths of length two\nAuthors: Qi Wu, Long-Tu Yuan\nDate: Mon Jul 20 16:16:56 2026\n\nWe determine the generalized Turán number of vertex-disjoint paths of length two and characterize all corresponding extremal graphs. Our proof combines the Lovász form of the Kruskal--Katona theorem with a discrete convexity argument.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn $p$-Spread Measures | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2609.08967)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; We show that if a set family is large under the product Bernoulli-$p$p measure, then no $p$p-spread measure can be supported on the family of sets not covered by the union of two of its members, answering the fractional version of Talagrand's discrete convexity problem. ... @misc{li2026pspreadmeasures, title={On $p$-Spread Measures}, author={Chen Li}, year={2026}, eprint={2609.08967}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08967}, }\nSubmitted 08 Sept 2026\n\n# On $p$p-Spread Measures\n\nCL\n\nChen Li\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study $p$p-spread probability measures on the Boolean lattice. We show that if a set family is large under the product Bernoulli-$p$p measure, then no $p$p-spread measure can be supported on the family of sets not covered by the union of two of its members, answering the fractional version of Talagrand's discrete convexity problem. Consequently, we establish two comparison theorems between $p$p-spread and product Bernoulli-$p$p measures.\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{li2026pspreadmeasures, title={On $p$-Spread Measures}, author={Chen Li}, year={2026}, eprint={2609.08967}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08967}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSymmetries, Graph Properties, and Quantum Speedups | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387095088_Symmetries_Graph_Properties_and_Quantum_Speedups)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Symmetries, Graph Properties, and Quantum Speedups ... We then analyze structured families of promises, including symmetric partial functions and promises supported on Hamming slices, obtaining sharp (up to polynomial factors) characterizations in terms of a single gap parameter for the symmetric case and refined slice-dependent bounds for k-slice domains. ... Finally, we apply this viewpoint to derive broad non-speedup criteria for some classes of functions admitting well-behaved completions, such as functions with low maximum influence on both the standard and p-biased hypercubes and functions with efficiently identifiable domains, and then show some hardness results for general completion techniques.\nFirst, we introduce promise versions of standard combinatorial measures (including block sensitivity and related variants) and prove that if the relevant promise and completion measures collapse, then deterministic and quantum query complexities are necessarily polynomially related, i.e., D(f)=poly(Q(f)). We then analyze structured families of promises, including symmetric partial functions and promises supported on Hamming slices, obtaining sharp (up to polynomial factors) characterizations in terms of a single gap parameter for the symmetric case and refined slice-dependent bounds for k-slice domains. Next, we formalize completion complexity as the minimum of a measure over total completions of a partial function, and show that completability of a measure captures the possibility of superpolynomial quantum speedups. Finally, we apply this viewpoint to derive broad non-speedup criteria for some classes of functions admitting well-behaved completions, such as functions with low maximum influence on both the standard and p-biased hypercubes and functions with efficiently identifiable domains, and then show some hardness results for general completion techniques.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn $p$-Spread Measures (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08967)\nciteturn3academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Tue Sep  8 16:19:44 2026 ... We show that if a set family is large under the product Bernoulli-$p$ measure, then no $p$-spread measure can be supported on the family of sets not covered by the union of two of its members, answering the fractional version of Talagrand's discrete convexity problem.\nTitle: On $p$-Spread Measures\nAuthors: Chen Li\nDate: Tue Sep  8 16:19:44 2026\n\nWe study $p$-spread probability measures on the Boolean lattice. We show that if a set family is large under the product Bernoulli-$p$ measure, then no $p$-spread measure can be supported on the family of sets not covered by the union of two of its members, answering the fractional version of Talagrand's discrete convexity problem. Consequently, we establish two comparison theorems between $p$-spread and product Bernoulli-$p$ measures.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nU.S. Patent Application for METHODS AND SYSTEMS FOR USING CALL DATA TO IDENTIFY INFLUENTIAL FORMER NETWORK SUBSCRIBERS Patent Application (Application #20260120132 issued April 30, 2026) - Justia Patents Search (https://patents.justia.com/patent/20260120132)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Other methods for defining “influence,” and then selecting for properties within the graph that correspond to such definitions, are contemplated by this disclosed technology.\n\nOther methods for defining “influence,” and then selecting for properties within the graph that correspond to such definitions, are contemplated by this disclosed technology. In some implementations, influence is a function of the number of connections a former member has with other former members. Influence can also be measured according to a weight that is applied to the graph, which scores some connections as more influential than others. For example, connections with a Primary Account Holder, or connections with a Corporate Account Holder, can be weighed more. The weight can be calculated according to the call data, or according to properties